<worksheet xml:id="ws_5_2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Practice Worksheet</title>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the exponential function graphed below. Then identify the growth factor and the growth rate.
</p>

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-3,xmax=4,
ymin=0,ymax=20,
xtick={-5,...,5},
ytick={-2,0,2,...,18},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(f(x)\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-5:5,thick] {8*(1.5)^x};
\node[label={0:{(2,18)}},circle,fill,inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:2,18) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
From the graph, the initial value is <m>A = 8</m>. Therefore we know that <m>f(x)=8b^x</m> and we need to determine <m>b</m>. To do so, plug in the other point <m>(2,18)</m>. This yields
<md>
18 = 8 b^2 \quad \implies \quad \frac{18}{8} =b^2 \quad \implies \quad b = 1.5.
</md> Therefore the function is given by
<md>
f(x) = 8 \cdot (1.5)^x.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the exponential function graphed below. Then identify the growth factor and the growth rate.
</p>

\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-1,xmax=5,
ymin=0,ymax=6,
xtick={-5,...,5},
ytick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(f(x)\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-5:5,thick] {4*(0.45)^x};
\node[label={45:{(3,0.3645)}},circle,fill,inner sep=2pt] at (axis cs:3,0.3645) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The initial value is <m>A=4</m>. So we know that <m>f(x)=4 b^x</m> where we need to determine <m>b</m>. To do so, plug in the other point <m>(3, 0.3645)</m> to get
<md>
0.3645 = 4 \cdot b^3 \quad \implies \quad 0.091125 = b^3 \quad \implies \quad b = 0.45.
</md> Thus the function is 
<md>
f(x) = 4 \cdot (0.45)^x.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>






<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the exponential function <m>f(t)=A\cdot b^t</m> passing through the ordered pairs <m>(0,6)</m> and <m>(4,16.2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The first point <m>(0,6)</m> means that the initial value is <m>A = 6</m>. Now, we know that <m>f(t) = 6 \cdot b^t</m> and we determine <m>b</m> now by plugging in the other point:
<md>
16.2 = 6 \cdot b^4 \quad \implies \quad 2.7 = b^4 \quad \implies \quad b = \sqrt[4]{2.7}=1.28186 .
</md> Thus the function is
<md>
f(t) = 6 (1.28186)^t.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the exponential function <m>g(x)</m> satisfying that <m>g(6)=10</m> and <m>g(8)=5</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that neither point is the initial value, so we need to create two equations and divide them. The equations are
<md>
10 = A \cdot b^6 \quad \text{and} \quad 5 = A \cdot b^8.
</md> Dividing them, we get
<md>
\frac{5}{10} = \frac{A\cdot b^8}{A\cdot b^6} \quad \implies \quad \frac{1}{2} = b^2 \quad \implies \quad b = \sqrt{\frac{1}{2}} =0.70711.
</md> Now, we can update the formula to
<md>
g(x) = A \cdot (0.70711)^x
</md> and solve for <m>A</m> by plugging in either of the given points. If we use the first one, say, then
<md>
10 = A (0.70711)^6 \quad \implies \quad A = 79.9978.
</md> So the equation is then
<md>
g(x) = 79.9978 \cdot (0.70711)^x.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the exponential function <m>h(t)</m> satisfying that <m>h(2)=1.2</m> and <m>h(4)=0.3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Note that neither point is the initial value, so we need to create two equations and divide them. The equations are
<md>
1.2 = A \cdot b^2 \quad \text{and} \quad 0.3 = A \cdot b^4.
</md> Dividing them, we get
<md>
\frac{0.3}{1.2}  = \frac{A\cdot b^4}{A\cdot b^2} \quad \implies \quad b^2 = 0.25 \quad \implies \quad b = \sqrt{0.25}  = 0.5.
</md> Now, we can update the formula to
<md>
h(t) = A \cdot (0.5)^t
</md> and solve for <m>A</m> by plugging in either of the given points. If we use the first one, say, then
<md>
1.2 = A (0.5)^2 \quad \implies \quad A = 4.8.
</md> So the function is then
<md>
h(t) = 4.8\cdot (0.5)^t.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


</worksheet>